@tgross35 My comments are all over, I didn't know about `@rustbot review` till recently, and I've been unsure how GitHub works. 

I am certain "That is, a `&T` to the inner value can never be obtained," is the correct phrasing.

I removed "Both of these rules ensure that there is never more than one reference pointing to the inner value.", as it implies a `&T` to the inner value might be hanging around or obtained; there is no `&T` to the inner value. While the sentence is true in that one `&mut T` can exist, the sentence is confusing. As you mentioned earlier, `&mut` is an exception, and Rust's normal behavior ensures there's only one. That is my reasoning. I will add the sentence back if you say so, and if so, please also say why so I can learn.

I'm really sorry; I've been a bit inactive recently and didn't notice this for some reason. For future reference, feel free to do `@rustbot reroll` to request a new reviewer if you don't hear back from the assignee by a few weeks after a ping.

In any case, this wording looks better, especially since `&T` is more general than `&mut T`. Thank you for following up with these improvements!

